{"parentUuid":"68a0a5b7-4adf-471e-90cc-f14d2e3c2781","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"8991ed8c-6615-4baa-8a4b-69a41242ec40","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, AskUserQuestion, Bash, Edit, Read, SendMessage, SendUserFile, Skill, TaskStop, ToolSearch, WebFetch, WebSearch, Workflow, Write, mcp__21st__get_component, mcp__21st__search, mcp__3f91f8d6-09ef-4d5b-b109-680b523e3682__hf_fs, mcp__51a58331-813c-4a2d-a9ae-d3085b62c965__creative_generate_speech, REDACTED, mcp__51a58331-813c-4a2d-a9ae-d3085b62c965__creative_get_generations, mcp__51a58331-813c-4a2d-a9ae-d3085b62c965__creative_list_voices, mcp__51a58331-813c-4a2d-a9ae-d3085b62c965__creative_transcribe_audio, mcp__Claude_Browser__browser_batch, mcp__Claude_Browser__computer, mcp__Claude_Browser__find, mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool, mcp__Claude_Browser__navigate, mcp__Claude_Browser__preview_list, mcp__Claude_Browser__preview_start, mcp__Claude_Browser__preview_stop, mcp__Claude_Browser__read_console_messages, mcp__Claude_Browser__read_network_requests, mcp__Claude_Browser__read_page, mcp__Claude_Browser__resize_window, mcp__Claude_Browser__tabs_context, mcp__Claude_Browser__tabs_select, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__create_static_site, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__create_web_service, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__get_deploy, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__list_deploys, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__list_logs, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__list_services, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__list_workspaces, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__trigger_deploy, mcp__a0b9ba12-83c6-4da1-b3bc-1beb1f33baea__update_environment_variables, mcp__ccd_session__start_session, mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan, mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up, mcp__devrelay-gateway__get_challenges, mcp__devrelay-gateway__get_knowledge_document, mcp__devrelay-gateway__list_event_offers, mcp__devrelay-gateway__list_my_mlh_events, mcp__devrelay-gateway__search_dev_to_semantic, mcp__terminal__list_terminal_tabs, mcp__terminal__read_terminal</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - **Ward:** HF26 Week 4 entry, a public MIT repo at github.com/MuhammadMurtuzaHussain/ward. Commit and push each verified change straight to main, with no branches.\n   - **Report v2:** the digest covers the child's answers (done).\n   - **Discord bot:** built in `apps/discord`.\n   - **Phases via agents:** stories, \"This is wrong\", the red team set, Give Ward to a friend, one-click desktop, governance, and the Ward Lite retrain. All done and merged.\n   - **Context second look for false alarms:** done, with E4B allowed to clear flags and E2B confirm-only.\n   - **Audit:** saved at `Hacktoberfest 26/ward-audit-2026-10-31.md`, kept out of the repo. Seven \"Now\" fixes merged.\n   - **User decisions:**\n     - Full help for unconfirmed dangers, with an \"unconfirmed\" report section.\n     - Discord server mode becomes a moderator tool, off by default.\n     - Preview release after a phone test.\n     - Install CTA from the start in the videos.\n     - Dangerous words that Gemma reads as a joke stay dangerous.\n   - **Animated series:**\n     - Roman Urdu scripts are in `ward-series/`.\n     - The user stopped at 3 videos: WARD-E1-KIDS, WARD-E6-KIDS and WARD-E6-TEEN. All are rendered with Meta's MMS Urdu TTS (CC-BY-NC) in `~/Projects/reel-factory`, with three local commits there, not pushed.\n     - Five other voiceovers are parked in `ward-series/voice/parked/`.\n   - **Render:**\n     - Static site ward (srv-dbj3vomc6qcc73bdg33g) at https://ward-w8cd.onrender.com.\n     - Bot ward-discord (srv-dbj49dgu01pc73eeg44g, Starter $7/mo, Frankfurt, commands use `corepack pnpm`), live as Ward#5111 with server mode off.\n     - The `/gemma` relay on the bot service uses `GEMMA_API_KEY` (the user's AI Studio key) with GEMMA_MODEL=gemma-4-31b-it.\n     - The site has env `VITE_GEMMA_DEMO_URL=https://ward-discord.onrender.com/gemma`.\n     - Workspace tea-davp1jm7bikc73etlheg.\n   - **Release:** v0.1.0 (preview) is published, and the signing secrets are set.\n   - **Latest request:** run the DigitalOcean GPU scorecard rerun (the user said \"go\", budget ~$2 of $3.71), and in parallel run security headers in a sub-session (started).\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **Monorepo:** pnpm, with packages/engine, apps/web, apps/discord, desktop, mobile, eval, train and viewer.\n   - **Engine:**\n     - `analyze` takes `SecondLook {name, canClear, review}` and `requireContext`.\n     - `Finding.unsure` and `Finding.secondLook`.\n     - `CAN_CLEAR=[\"gemma4:e4b\"]`.\n     - `GemmaSecondLook(transport, name, canClear)` and `ollamaTransport` (falls back on 501).\n     - A severity-3 flag in a 1:1 chat counts as backed up.\n     - A second look never clears severity 3.\n   - **Eval:**\n     - `eval/run.ts` systems include \"Gemma 4 E4B\", \"Gemma 4 E2B\" and \"ShieldGemma 2B\". `--only` re-runs named systems.\n     - `eval/data` (2.1 MB, gitignored) is needed on the droplet, plus `eval/results/scorecard.raw.json`.\n     - `pnpm redteam -- --gemma` re-runs the red team with Gemma.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **`apps/discord/src/gemmaDemo.ts`:** the relay.\n     - Allows only the ward-w8cd origin, with a 24 KB body limit and 40 requests per 10 minutes per IP.\n     - Picks the model via ListModels or GEMMA_MODEL.\n     - Filters `thought` parts and retries once on 5xx.\n     - Logs no text and stores nothing.\n   - **`apps/discord/src/main.ts`:** HTTP on `$PORT` serves `/gemma` plus a health check.\n   - **Web demo:**\n     - `apps/web/src/lib/demoGemma.ts` provides `DEMO_GEMMA_URL` and `demoTransport`.\n     - `run.ts` has `readWithDemoGemma(id)`.\n     - `Result.tsx` has `DemoGemmaOffer`.\n     - i18n: `result.demoGemmaTitle`, `demoGemmaLine` and `demoGemmaButton`, in en and ur.\n   - **Release:** README has a \"Try it in your browser\" badge, and the repo homepage is set to the Render URL.\n   - **Recent commits:** cac58b2, 4161602, a737edd, 5a1f6be, 2028a82.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **Reel factory:** the watcher raced a CLI render on old code. Fixed by restarting the stack with `launchctl kickstart` and rendering only via the watcher for new voiceovers.\n   - **Render bot build:** `corepack enable` hit EROFS. The user changed the commands to `corepack pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --filter \"@ward/discord...\"` and `corepack pnpm --filter @ward/discord start`.\n   - **Gemma relay:** intermittent 500s and thinking text in the output. Fixed with the thought filter and a retry.\n   - **Model choice:** gemma-4-26b-a4b-it was slower (376 s) and produced no usable verdicts, so I reverted to 31B (~118 s per 16-message chat).\n   - **Browser pane:** it blocks cross-origin calls to the relay, so I tested from Node with `scratchpad/relaytest.ts`. That run gave an urgent verdict, correct Gemma notes, and Zara (the upstander) at 0.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - **Gemma demo verified:**\n     - From Node against the live relay.\n     - A wrong origin gets 403.\n   - **Gemma demo not verified:** the live button flow in a real browser.\n   - **ElevenLabs:** 0 credits left.\n\n6. All user messages (in this period, condensed):\n   - \"Yes, cover them\"\n   - Questions on false alarms and context (\"i suppose it depends on context, can we pull in some way ai can see context and decide\").\n   - \"i will do the native-speaker review with a friend\"\n   - \"i tried discord app, works great and stuff, however after a few messages it stopped working.\"\n   - \"yeah if merged, remove the stuff.\"\n   - \"tell me whats needs to be done next, also tell me what needs testing and i will review it.\"\n   - \"Yes, build it\"\n   - The audit, make-it-amazing and TikTok request (\"plan and execute… ask me questions\").\n   - Answers to the setup questions:\n     - reels: \"A folder on this Mac\"\n     - audience: \"Both, separate cuts\"\n     - language: \"show me roman urdu script first then we move on\"\n     - survey: \"Later\"\n   - Policy answers: full help unconfirmed; moderator tool, off; preview after phone test; install CTA from the start.\n   - \"the series is good, let's get started. okay looks good decision. what exactly do you need from my side for the secrets.\"\n   - \"i wont be speaking anything, use my eleven labs connector see the remaining credits, and have it voiced from it.\"\n   - \"lets go with 2.\" (MMS)\n   - \"i think there some visual bugs with videos, see and fix that.\"\n   - \"Yes, do the rest\"\n   - \"let's stop with 3 videos\"\n   - \"now lets loop back to the originally planned partner technology… where is the project deployed btw\"\n   - \"tell me how to get on with render\"\n   - \"i connected the mcp, can you do the stuff now?\"\n   - \"can you pause other of my services on render, or bring them down to free tier and move this one to 7 dolla tier\"\n   - \"okay, create the bot\"\n   - \"i saved it, but there should be a real demo that is using gemma, can we use digital ocean or google ai studio for it\"\n   - Screenshot: \"this issue\"\n   - \"yes its up\"\n   - \"yeah its working.\"\n   - \"i think it is connected to the cli, i have 3.71 dollars in credits, is that enough?\"\n   - \"which project shall i add the key btw\"\n   - \"done\"\n   - \"whats the status\"\n   - \"Yes, make it faster\"\n   - \"whats next\"\n   - \"1. is done, whats next.\"\n   - \"go and let's start with security headers in a sub session as well in parallel.\"\n   - **Standing constraints:**\n     - Push verified changes to main with no branches.\n     - Use they/them for people.\n     - Never print `password.txt`.\n     - The signing key stays in `Hacktoberfest 26/ward-android-signing/`.\n     - Don't set secrets for the user; the user pastes the tokens and keys.\n     - Never publish to DEV without a yes.\n     - No ElevenLabs spend without asking.\n     - Child chats never leave the device, except the opt-in labelled demo.\n     - The audit file stays out of the repo.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - **DigitalOcean GPU run (in progress):** RTX 4000 Ada (`gpu-4000adax1-20gb`, $0.76/hour) with image `gpu-h100x1-base`, in tor1, atl1 or nyc2.\n     - Install Ollama and pull gemma4:e4b, gemma4:e2b and shieldgemma:2b.\n     - Install Node 24 and pnpm, clone the repo, and scp `eval/data` plus `eval/results/scorecard.raw.json`.\n     - Run `pnpm eval -- --only=\"Gemma 4 E4B,Gemma 4 E2B,ShieldGemma 2B\"` and `pnpm redteam -- --gemma`.\n     - Copy results back and update README and REDTEAM.\n     - **Destroy the droplet and delete the SSH key from DO**, keeping the spend at or under ~$2.\n   - **Headers sub-session:** ward-headers, local_47156514-d024-4c01-b309-cf4e61ad016b. Await its report and verify.\n   - **Later:**\n     - Re-import Entire.\n     - The DEV post, when the user says.\n     - Native-speaker review.\n     - The help-page checker (Browserbase/SerpApi).\n     - The audit's \"weeks\" items.\n     - Optional faster Gemma demo: parallel batches.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   - The headers sub-session was started.\n   - I then ran a Bash command that:\n     - generated `scratchpad/do_key` (ed25519, comment ward-gpu-run)\n     - imported it to DO as \"ward-gpu-run\", writing the ID to `scratchpad/do_key.id`\n     - looped over tor1, atl1 and nyc2 running `doctl compute droplet create ward-gpu-run --size gpu-4000adax1-20gb --image gpu-h100x1-base --ssh-keys <id> --tag-name ward-gpu-run`\n   - **That call was interrupted, with an unknown outcome.** A droplet may exist and be billing.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   - Immediately check state with `doctl compute droplet list --tag-name ward-gpu-run` and `doctl compute ssh-key list`.\n   - If no droplet exists, re-run the create, after first checking whether the key was already imported.\n   - Then provision and run the job, per the user's \"go\".\n   - Always destroy the droplet (`doctl compute droplet delete --tag-name ward-gpu-run -f`) and the key at the end.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/haider/.claude/projects/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"d992ba5a-9ab5-4c73-b045-2b12f5f2a1ef","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:20.896Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":46,"turnIndex":69},"turnOrigin":"human","summarizedTurnPromptUuids":["bea9cf1c-e29d-4cb7-8066-b11f87197dd5"],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"d992ba5a-9ab5-4c73-b045-2b12f5f2a1ef","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/src/gemmaDemo.ts","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/src/gemmaDemo.ts","content":"// The web demo's Gemma: a narrow relay from the Ward demo site to Gemma on Google AI Studio, so\n// judges and visitors can see a close read without installing anything. Real installs of Ward\n// never use it: they read on the device. The key stays here (GEMMA_API_KEY), never in the page.\n// It accepts only the demo site's origin, small bodies and a few requests per visitor, and it\n// logs no text and stores nothing.\n\nimport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n\nconst ORIGINS = (process.env.GEMMA_DEMO_ORIGINS ?? \"https://ward-w8cd.onrender.com\").split(\",\").map((s) => s.trim());\nconst API = \"https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta\";\nconst MAX_BODY = 24_000;\nconst WINDOW_MS = 10 * 60_000;\nconst PER_WINDOW = 40;\nconst seen = new Map<string, number[]>();\n\nlet model: Promise<string> | null = null;\n\n/** The newest instruction-tuned Gemma the key can use, unless GEMMA_MODEL names one. */\nfunction pickModel(key: string): Promise<string> {\n  model ??= (async () => {\n    if (process.env.GEMMA_MODEL) return process.env.GEMMA_MODEL;\n    const r = await fetch(`${API}/models?pageSize=200`, { headers: { \"x-goog-api-key\": key } });\n    const names = ((await r.json()) as { models?: { name: string }[] }).models?.map((m) => m.name.replace(/^models\\//, \"\")) ?? [];\n    const gemma = names.filter((n) => /^gemma-\\d/.test(n) && /-it$/.test(n));\n    const rank = (n: string) => {\n      const [, ver, size] = /^gemma-([\\d.]+)n?-?(?:e)?(\\d+)?/.exec(n) ?? [];\n      return Number(ver ?? 0) * 1000 + Math.min(Number(size ?? 0), 27);\n    };\n    const best = gemma.sort((a, b) => rank(b) - rank(a))[0] ?? \"gemma-3-27b-it\";\n    console.log(new Date().toISOString(), `gemma demo: using ${best} (available: ${gemma.join(\", \")})`);\n    return best;\n  })();\n  model.catch(() => (model = null));\n  return model;\n}\n\nfunction allowed(ip: string) {\n  const now = Date.now();\n  const hits = (seen.get(ip) ?? []).filter((t) => t > now - WINDOW_MS);\n  if (hits.length >= PER_WINDOW) return false;\n  hits.push(now);\n  seen.set(ip, hits);\n  if (seen.size > 5000) for (const [k, v] of seen) if (!v.some((t) => t > now - WINDOW_MS)) seen.delete(k);\n  return true;\n}\n\nfunction send(res: ServerResponse, origin: string | undefined, status: number, body: unknown) {\n  res.writeHead(status, {\n    \"content-type\": \"application/json\",\n    \"cache-control\": \"no-store\",\n    ...(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin) ? { \"access-control-allow-origin\": origin, vary: \"Origin\" } : {}),\n  });\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(body));\n}\n\n/** Handles /gemma. Returns false for any other path. */\nexport async function handleGemma(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse): Promise<boolean> {\n  if (!req.url?.startsWith(\"/gemma\")) return false;\n  const origin = req.headers.origin;\n  const key = process.env.GEMMA_API_KEY;\n  if (req.method === \"OPTIONS\") {\n    res.writeHead(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin) ? 204 : 403, {\n      ...(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin)\n        ? { \"access-control-allow-origin\": origin, \"access-control-allow-methods\": \"POST\", \"access-control-allow-headers\": \"content-type\", \"access-control-max-age\": \"600\", vary: \"Origin\" }\n        : {}),\n    });\n    res.end();\n    return true;\n  }\n  if (req.method !== \"POST\" || !origin || !ORIGINS.includes(origin)) return void send(res, origin, 403, { error: \"not allowed\" }), true;\n  if (!key) return void send(res, origin, 503, { error: \"the demo Gemma isn't configured\" }), true;\n  const ip = String(req.headers[\"x-forwarded-for\"] ?? req.socket.remoteAddress ?? \"\").split(\",\")[0]!.trim();\n  if (!allowed(ip)) return void send(res, origin, 429, { error: \"too many requests; try again in a few minutes\" }), true;\n\n  let raw = \"\";\n  for await (const chunk of req) {\n    raw += chunk;\n    if (raw.length > MAX_BODY) return void send(res, origin, 413, { error: \"too long for the demo\" }), true;\n  }\n  let prompt: string;\n  try {\n    const b = JSON.parse(raw) as { system?: unknown; user?: unknown };\n    if (typeof b.user !== \"string\") throw new Error();\n    // Gemma on the API takes no system instruction, so it leads the one user turn.\n    prompt = `${typeof b.system === \"string\" ? `${b.system}\\n\\n` : \"\"}${b.user}`;\n  } catch {\n    return void send(res, origin, 400, { error: \"bad request\" }), true;\n  }\n  try {\n    const m = await pickModel(key);\n    const call = () =>\n      fetch(`${API}/models/${m}:generateContent`, {\n        method: \"POST\",\n        headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\", \"x-goog-api-key\": key },\n        body: JSON.stringify({ contents: [{ role: \"user\", parts: [{ text: prompt }] }], generationConfig: { temperature: 0, maxOutputTokens: 4096 } }),\n      });\n    // AI Studio sometimes answers 5xx under load; one retry covers most of it.\n    let r = await call();\n    if (r.status >= 500) r = await call();\n    const j = (await r.json()) as { candidates?: { content?: { parts?: { text?: string; thought?: boolean }[] } }[]; error?: { message?: string } };\n    if (!r.ok) {\n      console.log(new Date().toISOString(), `gemma demo: upstream ${r.status}`);\n      return void send(res, origin, 502, { error: \"Gemma didn't answer\" }), true;\n    }\n    // Gemma 4 can return its thinking as separate parts; only the answer goes back.\n    const parts = j.candidates?.[0]?.content?.parts ?? [];\n    send(res, origin, 200, { text: parts.filter((p) => !p.thought).map((p) => p.text ?? \"\").join(\"\"), model: m });\n  } catch {\n    send(res, origin, 502, { error: \"Gemma didn't answer\" });\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n","numLines":113,"startLine":1,"totalLines":113}},"displayPath":"ward/apps/discord/src/gemmaDemo.ts"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"96d26881-be31-4eaa-9ff8-460aefe9c4fd","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.309Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/src/gemmaDemo.ts\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t// The web demo's Gemma: a narrow relay from the Ward demo site to Gemma on Google AI Studio, so\n2\t// judges and visitors can see a close read without installing anything. Real installs of Ward\n3\t// never use it: they read on the device. The key stays here (GEMMA_API_KEY), never in the page.\n4\t// It accepts only the demo site's origin, small bodies and a few requests per visitor, and it\n5\t// logs no text and stores nothing.\n6\t\n7\timport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n8\t\n9\tconst ORIGINS = (process.env.GEMMA_DEMO_ORIGINS ?? \"https://ward-w8cd.onrender.com\").split(\",\").map((s) => s.trim());\n10\tconst API = \"https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta\";\n11\tconst MAX_BODY = 24_000;\n12\tconst WINDOW_MS = 10 * 60_000;\n13\tconst PER_WINDOW = 40;\n14\tconst seen = new Map<string, number[]>();\n15\t\n16\tlet model: Promise<string> | null = null;\n17\t\n18\t/** The newest instruction-tuned Gemma the key can use, unless GEMMA_MODEL names one. */\n19\tfunction pickModel(key: string): Promise<string> {\n20\t  model ??= (async () => {\n21\t    if (process.env.GEMMA_MODEL) return process.env.GEMMA_MODEL;\n22\t    const r = await fetch(`${API}/models?pageSize=200`, { headers: { \"x-goog-api-key\": key } });\n23\t    const names = ((await r.json()) as { models?: { name: string }[] }).models?.map((m) => m.name.replace(/^models\\//, \"\")) ?? [];\n24\t    const gemma = names.filter((n) => /^gemma-\\d/.test(n) && /-it$/.test(n));\n25\t    const rank = (n: string) => {\n26\t      const [, ver, size] = /^gemma-([\\d.]+)n?-?(?:e)?(\\d+)?/.exec(n) ?? [];\n27\t      return Number(ver ?? 0) * 1000 + Math.min(Number(size ?? 0), 27);\n28\t    };\n29\t    const best = gemma.sort((a, b) => rank(b) - rank(a))[0] ?? \"gemma-3-27b-it\";\n30\t    console.log(new Date().toISOString(), `gemma demo: using ${best} (available: ${gemma.join(\", \")})`);\n31\t    return best;\n32\t  })();\n33\t  model.catch(() => (model = null));\n34\t  return model;\n35\t}\n36\t\n37\tfunction allowed(ip: string) {\n38\t  const now = Date.now();\n39\t  const hits = (seen.get(ip) ?? []).filter((t) => t > now - WINDOW_MS);\n40\t  if (hits.length >= PER_WINDOW) return false;\n41\t  hits.push(now);\n42\t  seen.set(ip, hits);\n43\t  if (seen.size > 5000) for (const [k, v] of seen) if (!v.some((t) => t > now - WINDOW_MS)) seen.delete(k);\n44\t  return true;\n45\t}\n46\t\n47\tfunction send(res: ServerResponse, origin: string | undefined, status: number, body: unknown) {\n48\t  res.writeHead(status, {\n49\t    \"content-type\": \"application/json\",\n50\t    \"cache-control\": \"no-store\",\n51\t    ...(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin) ? { \"access-control-allow-origin\": origin, vary: \"Origin\" } : {}),\n52\t  });\n53\t  res.end(JSON.stringify(body));\n54\t}\n55\t\n56\t/** Handles /gemma. Returns false for any other path. */\n57\texport async function handleGemma(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse): Promise<boolean> {\n58\t  if (!req.url?.startsWith(\"/gemma\")) return false;\n59\t  const origin = req.headers.origin;\n60\t  const key = process.env.GEMMA_API_KEY;\n61\t  if (req.method === \"OPTIONS\") {\n62\t    res.writeHead(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin) ? 204 : 403, {\n63\t      ...(origin && ORIGINS.includes(origin)\n64\t        ? { \"access-control-allow-origin\": origin, \"access-control-allow-methods\": \"POST\", \"access-control-allow-headers\": \"content-type\", \"access-control-max-age\": \"600\", vary: \"Origin\" }\n65\t        : {}),\n66\t    });\n67\t    res.end();\n68\t    return true;\n69\t  }\n70\t  if (req.method !== \"POST\" || !origin || !ORIGINS.includes(origin)) return void send(res, origin, 403, { error: \"not allowed\" }), true;\n71\t  if (!key) return void send(res, origin, 503, { error: \"the demo Gemma isn't configured\" }), true;\n72\t  const ip = String(req.headers[\"x-forwarded-for\"] ?? req.socket.remoteAddress ?? \"\").split(\",\")[0]!.trim();\n73\t  if (!allowed(ip)) return void send(res, origin, 429, { error: \"too many requests; try again in a few minutes\" }), true;\n74\t\n75\t  let raw = \"\";\n76\t  for await (const chunk of req) {\n77\t    raw += chunk;\n78\t    if (raw.length > MAX_BODY) return void send(res, origin, 413, { error: \"too long for the demo\" }), true;\n79\t  }\n80\t  let prompt: string;\n81\t  try {\n82\t    const b = JSON.parse(raw) as { system?: unknown; user?: unknown };\n83\t    if (typeof b.user !== \"string\") throw new Error();\n84\t    // Gemma on the API takes no system instruction, so it leads the one user turn.\n85\t    prompt = `${typeof b.system === \"string\" ? `${b.system}\\n\\n` : \"\"}${b.user}`;\n86\t  } catch {\n87\t    return void send(res, origin, 400, { error: \"bad request\" }), true;\n88\t  }\n89\t  try {\n90\t    const m = await pickModel(key);\n91\t    const call = () =>\n92\t      fetch(`${API}/models/${m}:generateContent`, {\n93\t        method: \"POST\",\n94\t        headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\", \"x-goog-api-key\": key },\n95\t        body: JSON.stringify({ contents: [{ role: \"user\", parts: [{ text: prompt }] }], generationConfig: { temperature: 0, maxOutputTokens: 4096 } }),\n96\t      });\n97\t    // AI Studio sometimes answers 5xx under load; one retry covers most of it.\n98\t    let r = await call();\n99\t    if (r.status >= 500) r = await call();\n100\t    const j = (await r.json()) as { candidates?: { content?: { parts?: { text?: string; thought?: boolean }[] } }[]; error?: { message?: string } };\n101\t    if (!r.ok) {\n102\t      console.log(new Date().toISOString(), `gemma demo: upstream ${r.status}`);\n103\t      return void send(res, origin, 502, { error: \"Gemma didn't answer\" }), true;\n104\t    }\n105\t    // Gemma 4 can return its thinking as separate parts; only the answer goes back.\n106\t    const parts = j.candidates?.[0]?.content?.parts ?? [];\n107\t    send(res, origin, 200, { text: parts.filter((p) => !p.thought).map((p) => p.text ?? \"\").join(\"\"), model: m });\n108\t  } catch {\n109\t    send(res, origin, 502, { error: \"Gemma didn't answer\" });\n110\t  }\n111\t  return true;\n112\t}\n113\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"96d26881-be31-4eaa-9ff8-460aefe9c4fd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/src/main.ts","displayPath":"ward/apps/discord/src/main.ts"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ac7617b1-2456-431b-8ca2-992dcaefc144","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.309Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/src/main.ts was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"ac7617b1-2456-431b-8ca2-992dcaefc144","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/haider/.claude/projects/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/memory/ward-series-reels.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/haider/.claude/projects/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/memory/ward-series-reels.md","content":"---\nname: ward-series-reels\ndescription: \"How Ward's animated awareness series is made in the owner's reel factory (voice, timing, rules)\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda\n  modified: 2026-10-31T18:15:48.582Z\n---\n\nWard's animated series (scripts in `Hacktoberfest 26/ward-series/`, Roman Urdu, kids + teen cuts) is rendered in the owner's reel factory at `~/Projects/reel-factory` (storyboards `data/scripts/WARD-*.json`, ghost cast kind, `chat` prop, `\"cta\": false`, per-reel `\"style\"` and `\"words\"`).\n\nAs of 2026-10-31 ElevenLabs has 0 credits (quota 146,725 used); the API key lacks User Read, so the balance can't be read. The owner won't record their voice, and chose Meta's open MMS Urdu TTS (`facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic`, CC-BY-NC 4.0, run with `ward/train/.venv`) via `ward-series/voice/voice_mms.py`, which also writes `<ID>.words.json` caption timing next to the voiceover.\n\n**Why:** the owner's reel-factory rule is \"only my clone or my recording\"; MMS was their explicit exception for this series.\n**How to apply:** for each episode write a lines JSON (roman, urdu, pause), voice it with voice_mms.py into `inbox/voiceovers/<ID>.wav`, switch off audio tighten/enhance in the storyboard's `style`, then `render`. Never spend ElevenLabs credits without asking. Related: [[ward-push-to-main]].\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}},"displayPath":"../../.claude/projects/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/memory/ward-series-reels.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4598b185-10dd-4020-9ddc-7699a1bef6ce","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.309Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/haider/.claude/projects/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/memory/ward-series-reels.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t---\n2\tname: ward-series-reels\n3\tdescription: \"How Ward's animated awareness series is made in the owner's reel factory (voice, timing, rules)\"\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: 0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda\n8\t  modified: 2026-10-31T18:15:48.582Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tWard's animated series (scripts in `Hacktoberfest 26/ward-series/`, Roman Urdu, kids + teen cuts) is rendered in the owner's reel factory at `~/Projects/reel-factory` (storyboards `data/scripts/WARD-*.json`, ghost cast kind, `chat` prop, `\"cta\": false`, per-reel `\"style\"` and `\"words\"`).\n12\t\n13\tAs of 2026-10-31 ElevenLabs has 0 credits (quota 146,725 used); the API key lacks User Read, so the balance can't be read. The owner won't record their voice, and chose Meta's open MMS Urdu TTS (`facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic`, CC-BY-NC 4.0, run with `ward/train/.venv`) via `ward-series/voice/voice_mms.py`, which also writes `<ID>.words.json` caption timing next to the voiceover.\n14\t\n15\t**Why:** the owner's reel-factory rule is \"only my clone or my recording\"; MMS was their explicit exception for this series.\n16\t**How to apply:** for each episode write a lines JSON (roman, urdu, pause), voice it with voice_mms.py into `inbox/voiceovers/<ID>.wav`, switch off audio tighten/enhance in the storyboard's `style`, then `render`. Never spend ElevenLabs credits without asking. Related: [[ward-push-to-main]].\n17\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"4598b185-10dd-4020-9ddc-7699a1bef6ce","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda/scratchpad/voice_mms.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda/scratchpad/voice_mms.py","content":"\"\"\"Voice a Ward episode with Meta's open MMS Urdu TTS, line by line, and write word timing for the\nRoman Urdu captions. Usage: python voice_mms.py <lines.json> <out.wav>\nlines.json: [{\"roman\": \"...\", \"urdu\": \"...\", \"pause\": seconds_after}, ...]\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport re\nimport sys\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.io.wavfile as wavfile\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, VitsModel\n\nlines = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\nout_wav = sys.argv[2]\ntorch.manual_seed(7)\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic\")\nmodel = VitsModel.from_pretrained(\"facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic\")\nmodel.speaking_rate = 0.92  # a little slower, for children\nsr = model.config.sampling_rate\n\n\ndef norm(w):\n    return re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", w.lower().replace(\"’\", \"'\"))\n\n\naudio, words, t = [np.zeros(int(0.35 * sr), dtype=np.float32)], [], 0.35\nfor ln in lines:\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        wav = model(**tok(ln[\"urdu\"], return_tensors=\"pt\")).waveform[0].numpy()\n    # trim the model's own leading/trailing near-silence so the timing is tight\n    idx = np.where(np.abs(wav) > 0.02)[0]\n    if len(idx):\n        wav = wav[max(0, idx[0] - int(0.03 * sr)): idx[-1] + int(0.05 * sr)]\n    dur = len(wav) / sr\n    toks = [w for w in ln[\"roman\"].split() if norm(w)]\n    weights = [len(norm(w)) + 1.5 for w in toks]\n    acc, total = 0.0, sum(weights)\n    for w, wt in zip(toks, weights):\n        a = t + acc / total * dur\n        acc += wt\n        words.append({\"text\": w, \"start\": round(a, 3), \"end\": round(t + acc / total * dur, 3)})\n    audio += [wav, np.zeros(int(ln[\"pause\"] * sr), dtype=np.float32)]\n    t += dur + ln[\"pause\"]\n    print(f\"{dur:5.2f}s  {ln['roman']}\")\n\npcm = np.concatenate(audio)\npcm = pcm / max(1e-6, np.abs(pcm).max()) * 0.9\nwavfile.write(out_wav, sr, (pcm * 32767).astype(np.int16))\njson.dump(words, open(out_wav.rsplit(\".\", 1)[0] + \".words.json\", \"w\"), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)\nprint(f\"total {t:.2f}s, {len(words)} words\")\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}},"displayPath":"../../../../private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda/scratchpad/voice_mms.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"60555340-2ac9-4f12-8565-01fcd291032c","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.309Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-haider-Desktop-Hacktoberfest-26/0cf62ca5-911b-48de-86c0-63490519bbda/scratchpad/voice_mms.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Voice a Ward episode with Meta's open MMS Urdu TTS, line by line, and write word timing for the\n2\tRoman Urdu captions. Usage: python voice_mms.py <lines.json> <out.wav>\n3\tlines.json: [{\"roman\": \"...\", \"urdu\": \"...\", \"pause\": seconds_after}, ...]\"\"\"\n4\timport json\n5\timport re\n6\timport sys\n7\t\n8\timport numpy as np\n9\timport scipy.io.wavfile as wavfile\n10\timport torch\n11\tfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, VitsModel\n12\t\n13\tlines = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\n14\tout_wav = sys.argv[2]\n15\ttorch.manual_seed(7)\n16\ttok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic\")\n17\tmodel = VitsModel.from_pretrained(\"facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic\")\n18\tmodel.speaking_rate = 0.92  # a little slower, for children\n19\tsr = model.config.sampling_rate\n20\t\n21\t\n22\tdef norm(w):\n23\t    return re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", w.lower().replace(\"’\", \"'\"))\n24\t\n25\t\n26\taudio, words, t = [np.zeros(int(0.35 * sr), dtype=np.float32)], [], 0.35\n27\tfor ln in lines:\n28\t    with torch.no_grad():\n29\t        wav = model(**tok(ln[\"urdu\"], return_tensors=\"pt\")).waveform[0].numpy()\n30\t    # trim the model's own leading/trailing near-silence so the timing is tight\n31\t    idx = np.where(np.abs(wav) > 0.02)[0]\n32\t    if len(idx):\n33\t        wav = wav[max(0, idx[0] - int(0.03 * sr)): idx[-1] + int(0.05 * sr)]\n34\t    dur = len(wav) / sr\n35\t    toks = [w for w in ln[\"roman\"].split() if norm(w)]\n36\t    weights = [len(norm(w)) + 1.5 for w in toks]\n37\t    acc, total = 0.0, sum(weights)\n38\t    for w, wt in zip(toks, weights):\n39\t        a = t + acc / total * dur\n40\t        acc += wt\n41\t        words.append({\"text\": w, \"start\": round(a, 3), \"end\": round(t + acc / total * dur, 3)})\n42\t    audio += [wav, np.zeros(int(ln[\"pause\"] * sr), dtype=np.float32)]\n43\t    t += dur + ln[\"pause\"]\n44\t    print(f\"{dur:5.2f}s  {ln['roman']}\")\n45\t\n46\tpcm = np.concatenate(audio)\n47\tpcm = pcm / max(1e-6, np.abs(pcm).max()) * 0.9\n48\twavfile.write(out_wav, sr, (pcm * 32767).astype(np.int16))\n49\tjson.dump(words, open(out_wav.rsplit(\".\", 1)[0] + \".words.json\", \"w\"), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)\n50\tprint(f\"total {t:.2f}s, {len(words)} words\")\n51\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"60555340-2ac9-4f12-8565-01fcd291032c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/README.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/README.md","content":"# Ward for Discord\n\nA friendly ghost in Discord for teens dealing with bullying. It runs the same open engine as the\nWard app (word lists for English, Roman Urdu, Hinglish and Bangla, plus the Ward Lite model on\nthe CPU), with no closed AI service anywhere.\n\nDiscord is for people 13 and over, so this is Ward for its 14 to 17 band (and 13 year olds).\n\n## What it does\n\n**Personal mode** (a teen installs Ward to their own account; works in every server and DM):\n\n- **Right-click a message → Apps → Check with Ward.** Is it bullying, what kind, and what to do\n  on Discord: save proof, report, block, who to tell. Only the teen sees the answer.\n- **Right-click → Apps → Save as evidence.** Ward DMs the teen a copy with a SHA-256\n  fingerprint taken at that moment (the same `sender|time|text` fingerprint the app's vault\n  takes). The DMs are the evidence store: no database, and nobody can edit a message Ward sent.\n- **`/ward report`.** Builds a [`ward.report/v2`](../../docs/REPORT.md) from everything the teen\n  saved, and DMs them `ward-report.json`. A counsellor (Ward's counsellor mode) or helpline (the\n  [case viewer](../../viewer/ward-viewer.html)) can check every message wasn't changed.\n- **`/ward help`.** Free, private helplines by country, Take It Down, and Discord's safety centre.\n\n**Server mode** is a moderator tool, **off by default**. The Ward app never alerts adults; server\nmode does, so it has its own policy ([SAFEGUARDING.md section 13](../../docs/SAFEGUARDING.md#13-ward-for-discord))\nand needs a safeguarding review before it's offered to anyone publicly. It runs only when the host\nsets `WARD_SERVER_MODE=on`, and then only in a server whose moderators create a private\n`#ward-alerts` channel that only they and Ward can see. Without the setting, Ward doesn't ask\nDiscord for the Message Content intent and can't read server messages at all.\n\n- Dangerous messages (threats, urging self-harm, sexual pressure, threats to share private images\n  or details) go to `#ward-alerts` with a link. For self-harm, or when Ward can't tell the kind,\n  the alert has the link, channel and kind only, never the words or the sender: it may be someone\n  asking for help. A person decides. Ward never deletes, mutes or bans.\n- Before any nudge or check-in, Ward checks whether the writer is telling what happened to them\n  (\"someone told me to kys\", \"usne mujhe bola\", \"আমাকে বলেছে\") or standing up for someone (\"stop\n  telling Sara to kys\"). Then nobody gets a DM, and a dangerous one reaches moderators only as\n  \"someone may need support\", with the link and without the writer's name. Because that hides\n  who wrote it, the check is narrow: a bare \"someone said\", or quotation marks alone, don't count,\n  and nor does a message that also says \"you\" (\"tu\", \"তোকে\") or \"or else\" (\"warna\", \"নইলে\"),\n  because that is how threats read. Those alert as usual, but if they might be a report, the\n  writer still never gets a nudge.\n- The sender of a hurtful (not dangerous) message gets a private nudge to edit or delete it\n  (`ward: no-nudge` in the channel topic turns this off). Never for a dangerous message: it would\n  tip off someone making threats.\n- Whoever it was aimed at, by mention or reply, gets a short, kind check-in (`ward: no-checkin`\n  turns this off).\n- `/ward server` explains all this to moderators and gives them a notice to pin for members.\n\n## What it keeps\n\nNothing. No database, no message logs (it logs errors, its own start-up line, and what it did,\nnever what anyone wrote). Personal mode reads only the message a teen picks; Discord sends it with\nthe command. Server mode, where it's on, reads messages in servers that created `#ward-alerts`, in\nmemory, and forgets them. Its alerts are ordinary Discord messages, though: they stay in\n`#ward-alerts` until a moderator deletes them.\n\nUnlike the Ward app, the bot does run on someone's computer, not the teen's phone: whoever hosts\nit. That's why it's open source and one command to self-host. A school or a server can read the\ncode and run its own copy.\n\n## Run your own\n\n1. **Create the app** at <https://discord.com/developers/applications> → New Application.\n   - **Installation:** tick **User Install** and **Guild Install**. Under Default Install\n     Settings, Guild Install scopes: `applications.commands`, `bot`; permissions: *View\n     Channels*, *Send Messages*, *Embed Links*, *Read Message History*. User Install scope:\n     `applications.commands`.\n   - **Bot:** reset the token and copy it. Only if you run server mode (see above), switch on\n     **Message Content Intent**. Leave *Public Bot* on if anyone may install it.\n   - Copy the **Application ID** from General Information.\n2. **Configure:** `cp apps/discord/.env.example apps/discord/.env` and fill in `DISCORD_TOKEN`\n   and `DISCORD_APP_ID`. Never commit `.env`.\n3. **Register the commands** (once, and after changing `src/commands.ts`):\n   `pnpm --filter @ward/discord register`\n4. **Start it:** `pnpm --filter @ward/discord start`\n5. **Install it:** the Installation page's install link. Pick *Try it now* / *Add to My Apps*\n   for personal mode, or *Add to Server* for server mode.\n\nCommands can take a few minutes to appear the first time.\n\n**On Render:** [`render.yaml`](../../render.yaml) at the repo root describes a background worker.\nNew → Blueprint → this repo, then set `DISCORD_TOKEN` and `DISCORD_APP_ID` in the dashboard. The\nblueprint pins `WARD_SERVER_MODE=off`, so a public instance runs personal mode only.\n\nServer mode sends one nudge per sender every 10 minutes and one check-in per person every hour,\nso a burst of messages isn't a burst of DMs. While testing, set `WARD_NUDGE_MINUTES=0` and\n`WARD_CHECKIN_MINUTES=0`, or it will look as if Ward stopped after the first one. The bot logs\nwhat it did (never what anyone wrote) to the terminal.\n\n**Context.** Ward Lite reads one message at a time, so on its own it can mistake \"I'll find you at\nthe fair\" for a threat. When only Ward Lite flagged a message, the bot reads the eight messages\nbefore it (where it can see the channel): if nothing around it backs the flag up, Ward says it\nisn't sure and server mode stays quiet. If the host runs Gemma through Ollama\n(`WARD_GEMMA_MODEL=gemma4:e4b`), Gemma takes a second look with that context and can clear false\nalarms; on the everyday and red-team sets it cleared 22 of 24 false alarms and 2 of 46 hurtful\nmessages, and no dangerous ones ([eval/secondlook](../../eval/secondlook/results.md)).\n\n`WARD_SERVER_MODE=on` switches server mode on (it's off otherwise, with no Message Content intent).\n`WARD_LITE=off` reads with the word lists only.\n\n## Develop\n\n```bash\npnpm --filter @ward/discord test       # engine readings, evidence round trip, report verifies, server mode\npnpm --filter @ward/discord typecheck\n```\n","numLines":107,"startLine":1,"totalLines":107}},"displayPath":"ward/apps/discord/README.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"be84538e-d087-40ec-b1a6-731b068e3170","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.309Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward/apps/discord/README.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Ward for Discord\n2\t\n3\tA friendly ghost in Discord for teens dealing with bullying. It runs the same open engine as the\n4\tWard app (word lists for English, Roman Urdu, Hinglish and Bangla, plus the Ward Lite model on\n5\tthe CPU), with no closed AI service anywhere.\n6\t\n7\tDiscord is for people 13 and over, so this is Ward for its 14 to 17 band (and 13 year olds).\n8\t\n9\t## What it does\n10\t\n11\t**Personal mode** (a teen installs Ward to their own account; works in every server and DM):\n12\t\n13\t- **Right-click a message → Apps → Check with Ward.** Is it bullying, what kind, and what to do\n14\t  on Discord: save proof, report, block, who to tell. Only the teen sees the answer.\n15\t- **Right-click → Apps → Save as evidence.** Ward DMs the teen a copy with a SHA-256\n16\t  fingerprint taken at that moment (the same `sender|time|text` fingerprint the app's vault\n17\t  takes). The DMs are the evidence store: no database, and nobody can edit a message Ward sent.\n18\t- **`/ward report`.** Builds a [`ward.report/v2`](../../docs/REPORT.md) from everything the teen\n19\t  saved, and DMs them `ward-report.json`. A counsellor (Ward's counsellor mode) or helpline (the\n20\t  [case viewer](../../viewer/ward-viewer.html)) can check every message wasn't changed.\n21\t- **`/ward help`.** Free, private helplines by country, Take It Down, and Discord's safety centre.\n22\t\n23\t**Server mode** is a moderator tool, **off by default**. The Ward app never alerts adults; server\n24\tmode does, so it has its own policy ([SAFEGUARDING.md section 13](../../docs/SAFEGUARDING.md#13-ward-for-discord))\n25\tand needs a safeguarding review before it's offered to anyone publicly. It runs only when the host\n26\tsets `WARD_SERVER_MODE=on`, and then only in a server whose moderators create a private\n27\t`#ward-alerts` channel that only they and Ward can see. Without the setting, Ward doesn't ask\n28\tDiscord for the Message Content intent and can't read server messages at all.\n29\t\n30\t- Dangerous messages (threats, urging self-harm, sexual pressure, threats to share private images\n31\t  or details) go to `#ward-alerts` with a link. For self-harm, or when Ward can't tell the kind,\n32\t  the alert has the link, channel and kind only, never the words or the sender: it may be someone\n33\t  asking for help. A person decides. Ward never deletes, mutes or bans.\n34\t- Before any nudge or check-in, Ward checks whether the writer is telling what happened to them\n35\t  (\"someone told me to kys\", \"usne mujhe bola\", \"আমাকে বলেছে\") or standing up for someone (\"stop\n36\t  telling Sara to kys\"). Then nobody gets a DM, and a dangerous one reaches moderators only as\n37\t  \"someone may need support\", with the link and without the writer's name. Because that hides\n38\t  who wrote it, the check is narrow: a bare \"someone said\", or quotation marks alone, don't count,\n39\t  and nor does a message that also says \"you\" (\"tu\", \"তোকে\") or \"or else\" (\"warna\", \"নইলে\"),\n40\t  because that is how threats read. Those alert as usual, but if they might be a report, the\n41\t  writer still never gets a nudge.\n42\t- The sender of a hurtful (not dangerous) message gets a private nudge to edit or delete it\n43\t  (`ward: no-nudge` in the channel topic turns this off). Never for a dangerous message: it would\n44\t  tip off someone making threats.\n45\t- Whoever it was aimed at, by mention or reply, gets a short, kind check-in (`ward: no-checkin`\n46\t  turns this off).\n47\t- `/ward server` explains all this to moderators and gives them a notice to pin for members.\n48\t\n49\t## What it keeps\n50\t\n51\tNothing. No database, no message logs (it logs errors, its own start-up line, and what it did,\n52\tnever what anyone wrote). Personal mode reads only the message a teen picks; Discord sends it with\n53\tthe command. Server mode, where it's on, reads messages in servers that created `#ward-alerts`, in\n54\tmemory, and forgets them. Its alerts are ordinary Discord messages, though: they stay in\n55\t`#ward-alerts` until a moderator deletes them.\n56\t\n57\tUnlike the Ward app, the bot does run on someone's computer, not the teen's phone: whoever hosts\n58\tit. That's why it's open source and one command to self-host. A school or a server can read the\n59\tcode and run its own copy.\n60\t\n61\t## Run your own\n62\t\n63\t1. **Create the app** at <https://discord.com/developers/applications> → New Application.\n64\t   - **Installation:** tick **User Install** and **Guild Install**. Under Default Install\n65\t     Settings, Guild Install scopes: `applications.commands`, `bot`; permissions: *View\n66\t     Channels*, *Send Messages*, *Embed Links*, *Read Message History*. User Install scope:\n67\t     `applications.commands`.\n68\t   - **Bot:** reset the token and copy it. Only if you run server mode (see above), switch on\n69\t     **Message Content Intent**. Leave *Public Bot* on if anyone may install it.\n70\t   - Copy the **Application ID** from General Information.\n71\t2. **Configure:** `cp apps/discord/.env.example apps/discord/.env` and fill in `DISCORD_TOKEN`\n72\t   and `DISCORD_APP_ID`. Never commit `.env`.\n73\t3. **Register the commands** (once, and after changing `src/commands.ts`):\n74\t   `pnpm --filter @ward/discord register`\n75\t4. **Start it:** `pnpm --filter @ward/discord start`\n76\t5. **Install it:** the Installation page's install link. Pick *Try it now* / *Add to My Apps*\n77\t   for personal mode, or *Add to Server* for server mode.\n78\t\n79\tCommands can take a few minutes to appear the first time.\n80\t\n81\t**On Render:** [`render.yaml`](../../render.yaml) at the repo root describes a background worker.\n82\tNew → Blueprint → this repo, then set `DISCORD_TOKEN` and `DISCORD_APP_ID` in the dashboard. The\n83\tblueprint pins `WARD_SERVER_MODE=off`, so a public instance runs personal mode only.\n84\t\n85\tServer mode sends one nudge per sender every 10 minutes and one check-in per person every hour,\n86\tso a burst of messages isn't a burst of DMs. While testing, set `WARD_NUDGE_MINUTES=0` and\n87\t`WARD_CHECKIN_MINUTES=0`, or it will look as if Ward stopped after the first one. The bot logs\n88\twhat it did (never what anyone wrote) to the terminal.\n89\t\n90\t**Context.** Ward Lite reads one message at a time, so on its own it can mistake \"I'll find you at\n91\tthe fair\" for a threat. When only Ward Lite flagged a message, the bot reads the eight messages\n92\tbefore it (where it can see the channel): if nothing around it backs the flag up, Ward says it\n93\tisn't sure and server mode stays quiet. If the host runs Gemma through Ollama\n94\t(`WARD_GEMMA_MODEL=gemma4:e4b`), Gemma takes a second look with that context and can clear false\n95\talarms; on the everyday and red-team sets it cleared 22 of 24 false alarms and 2 of 46 hurtful\n96\tmessages, and no dangerous ones ([eval/secondlook](../../eval/secondlook/results.md)).\n97\t\n98\t`WARD_SERVER_MODE=on` switches server mode on (it's off otherwise, with no Message Content intent).\n99\t`WARD_LITE=off` reads with the word lists only.\n100\t\n101\t## Develop\n102\t\n103\t```bash\n104\tpnpm --filter @ward/discord test       # engine readings, evidence round trip, report verifies, server mode\n105\tpnpm --filter @ward/discord typecheck\n106\t```\n107\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
{"parentUuid":"be84538e-d087-40ec-b1a6-731b068e3170","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"design-taste-frontend","path":"userSettings:design-taste-frontend","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/haider/.claude/skills/design-taste-frontend\n\n# tasteskill: Anti-Slop Frontend Skill\n\n> Landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns. Not dashboards, not data tables, not multi-step product UI.\n> Every rule below is **contextual**. None of it fires automatically. First read the brief, then pull only what fits.\n\n---\n\n## 0. BRIEF INFERENCE (Read the Room Before Anything Else)\n\nBefore touching code or tweaking dials, **infer what the user actually wants**. Most LLM design output is bad because the model jumps to a default aesthetic instead of reading the room.\n\n### 0.A Read these signals first\n1. **Page kind** - landing (SaaS / consumer / agency / event), portfolio (dev / designer / creative studio), redesign (preserve vs overhaul), editorial / blog.\n2. **Vibe words** the user used - \"minimalist\", \"calm\", \"Linear-style\", \"Awwwards\", \"brutalist\", \"premium consumer\", \"Apple-y\", \"playful\", \"serious B2B\", \"editorial\", \"agency-y\", \"glassy\", \"dark tech\".\n3. **Reference signals** - URLs they linked, screenshots they pasted, products they named, brands they're competing with.\n4. **Audience** - B2B procurement panel vs. design-conscious consumer vs. recruiter scanning a portfolio. The audience picks the aesthetic, not your taste.\n5. **Brand assets that already exist** - logo, color, type, photography. For redesigns, these are starting material, not optional input (see Section 11).\n6. **Quiet constraints** - accessibility-first audiences, public-sector, regulated industries, trust-first commerce, kids' products. These constraints OVERRIDE aesthetic preference.\n\n### 0.B Output a one-line \"Design Read\" before generating\nBefore any code, state in one line: **\"Reading this as: \\<page kind> for \\<audience>, with a \\<vibe> language, leaning toward \\<design system or aesthetic family>.\"**\n\nExample reads:\n- *\"Reading this as: B2B SaaS landing for technical buyers, with a Linear-style minimalist language, leaning toward Tailwind utilities + Geist + restrained motion.\"*\n- *\"Reading this as: solo designer portfolio for hiring managers, with an editorial / kinetic-type language, leaning toward native CSS + scroll-driven animation + custom typography.\"*\n- *\"Reading this as: redesign of a public-sector service site, with a trust-first language, leaning toward GOV.UK Frontend or USWDS.\"*\n\n### 0.C If the brief is ambiguous, ask one question, do not guess\nAsk exactly **one** clarifying question - never a multi-question dump - and only when the design read genuinely diverges. Example: *\"Should this feel closer to Linear-clean or Awwwards-experimental?\"*\n\nIf you can confidently infer from context, **do not ask**. Just declare the design read and proceed.\n\n### 0.D Anti-Default Discipline\nDo not default to: AI-purple gradients, centered hero over dark mesh, three equal feature cards, generic glassmorphism on everything, infinite-loop micro-animations everywhere, Inter + slate-900. These are the LLM defaults. Reach past them deliberately based on the design read.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE THREE DIALS (Core Configuration)\n\nAfter the design read, set three dials. Every layout, motion, and density decision below is gated by these.\n\n* **`DESIGN_VARIANCE: 8`** - 1 = Perfect Symmetry, 10 = Artsy Chaos\n* **`MOTION_INTENSITY: 6`** - 1 = Static, 10 = Cinematic / Physics\n* **`VISUAL_DENSITY: 4`** - 1 = Art Gallery / Airy, 10 = Cockpit / Packed Data\n\n**Baseline:** `8 / 6 / 4`. Use these unless the design read overrides them. Do not ask the user to edit this file - overrides happen conversationally.\n\n### 1.A Dial Inference (design read → dial values)\n| Signal | VARIANCE | MOTION | DENSITY |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"minimalist / clean / calm / editorial / Linear-style\" | 5-6 | 3-4 | 2-3 |\n| \"premium consumer / Apple-y / luxury / brand\" | 7-8 | 5-7 | 3-4 |\n| \"playful / wild / Dribbble / Awwwards / experimental / agency\" | 9-10 | 8-10 | 3-4 |\n| \"landing page / portfolio / marketing site (default)\" | 7-9 | 6-8 | 3-5 |\n| \"trust-first / public-sector / regulated / accessibility-critical\" | 3-4 | 2-3 | 4-5 |\n| \"redesign - preserve\" | match existing | +1 | match existing |\n| \"redesign - overhaul\" | +2 | +2 | match existing |\n\n### 1.B Use-Case Presets\n| Use case | VARIANCE | MOTION | DENSITY |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Landing (SaaS, mainstream) | 7 | 6 | 4 |\n| Landing (Agency / creative) | 9 | 8 | 3 |\n| Landing (Premium consumer) | 7 | 6 | 3 |\n| Portfolio (Designer / studio) | 8 | 7 | 3 |\n| Portfolio (Developer) | 6 | 5 | 4 |\n| Editorial / Blog | 6 | 4 | 3 |\n| Public-sector service | 3 | 2 | 5 |\n| Redesign - preserve | match | match+1 | match |\n| Redesign - overhaul | +2 | +2 | match |\n\n### 1.C How the Dials Drive Output\nUse these (or user-overridden values) as global variables. Cross-references throughout this document refer to these exact variable names - never invent aliases like `LAYOUT_VARIANCE` or `ANIM_LEVEL`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. BRIEF → DESIGN SYSTEM MAP\n\nOnce you have the design read (Section 0) and dials (Section 1), pick the right foundation. Do not invent CSS for things that have an official package. Do not pretend an aesthetic trend is an official system.\n\n### 2.A When to reach for a real design system (use official packages)\n| Brief reads as… | Reach for | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Microsoft / enterprise SaaS / dashboards | `@fluentui/react-components` or `@fluentui/web-components` | Official Fluent UI, Microsoft tokens, accessibility done |\n| Google-ish UI, Material-flavored product | `@material/web` + Material 3 tokens | Official, theme-able via Material Theming |\n| IBM-style B2B / enterprise analytics | `@carbon/react` + `@carbon/styles` | Official Carbon, mature data-density patterns |\n| Shopify app surfaces | `polaris.js` web components / Polaris React | Required for Shopify admin UI |\n| Atlassian / Jira-style product | `@atlaskit/*` + `@atlaskit/tokens` | Official Atlassian DS |\n| GitHub-style devtool / community page | `@primer/css` or `@primer/react-brand` | Official Primer; Brand variant for marketing |\n| Public-sector UK service | `govuk-frontend` | Legally / regulatorily expected |\n| US public-sector / trust-first | `uswds` | Same |\n| Fast local-business / agency MVP | Bootstrap 5.3 | Boring, fast, works |\n| Modern accessible React foundation | `@radix-ui/themes` | Primitives + polished theme |\n| Modern SaaS where you own the components | shadcn/ui (`npx shadcn@latest add ...`) | You own the code, easy to customise; never ship default state |\n| Tailwind-based modern SaaS / AI marketing | Tailwind v4 utilities + `dark:` variant | Default for indie + small team builds |\n\n**Honesty rule:** if the brief reads as one of the systems above, install and use the **official** package. Do not recreate its CSS by hand. Do not import a system's tokens but then override 90% of them.\n\n**One system per project.** Do not mix Fluent React with Carbon in the same tree. Do not import shadcn/ui components into a Material 3 app.\n\n### 2.B When the brief is an aesthetic, not a system\nFor these directions, there is **no single official package**. Build with native CSS + Tailwind + a maintained component library. Be honest in code comments about what is borrowed inspiration vs. official material.\n\n| Aesthetic | Honest implementation |\n|---|---|\n| Glassmorphism / \"frosted glass\" | `backdrop-filter`, layered borders, highlight overlays. Provide solid-fill fallback for `prefers-reduced-transparency`. |\n| Bento (Apple-style tile grids) | CSS Grid with mixed cell sizes. No single library owns this. |\n| Brutalism | Native CSS, monospace, raw borders. No library. |\n| Editorial / magazine | Serif type, asymmetric grid, generous whitespace. No library. |\n| Dark tech / hacker | Mono + accent neon, terminal motifs. No library. |\n| Aurora / mesh gradients | SVG or layered radial gradients. No library. |\n| Kinetic typography | Native CSS animations, scroll-driven animations, GSAP for hijacks. No library. |\n| **Apple Liquid Glass** | Apple documents this for Apple platforms only. **There is no official `liquid-glass.css`.** Web implementations are approximations using `backdrop-filter` + layered borders + highlights. Label clearly as approximation. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. DEFAULT ARCHITECTURE & CONVENTIONS\n\nUnless the design read picks a real design system (Section 2.A), these are the defaults:\n\n### 3.A Stack\n* **Framework:** React or Next.js. Default to Server Components (RSC).\n  * **RSC SAFETY:** Global state works ONLY in Client Components. In Next.js, wrap providers in a `\"use client\"` component.\n  * **INTERACTIVITY ISOLATION:** Any component using Motion, scroll listeners, or pointer physics MUST be an isolated leaf with `'use client'` at the top. Server Components render static layouts only.\n* **Styling:** **Tailwind v4** (default). Tailwind v3 only if the existing project demands it.\n  * For v4: do NOT use `tailwindcss` plugin in `postcss.config.js`. Use `@tailwindcss/postcss` or the Vite plugin.\n* **Animation:** **Motion** (the library formerly known as Framer Motion). Import from `motion/react` (`import { motion } from \"motion/react\"`). The `framer-motion` package still works as a legacy alias - prefer `motion/react` in new code.\n* **Fonts:** Always use `next/font` (Next.js) or self-host with `@font-face` + `font-display: swap`. Never link Google Fonts via `<link>` in production.\n\n### 3.B State\n* Local `useState` / `useReducer` for isolated UI.\n* Global state ONLY for deep prop-drilling avoidance - Zustand, Jotai, or React context.\n* **NEVER** use `useState` to track continuous values driven by user input (mouse position, scroll progress, pointer physics, magnetic hover). Use Motion's `useMotionValue` / `useTransform` / `useScroll`. `useState` re-renders the React tree on every change and collapses on mobile.\n\n### 3.C Icons\n* **Allowed libraries (priority order):** `@phosphor-icons/react`, `hugeicons-react`, `@radix-ui/react-icons`, `@tabler/icons-react`.\n* **Discouraged:** `lucide-react`. Acceptable only when the user explicitly asks for it or the project already depends on it.\n* **NEVER hand-roll SVG icons.** If a glyph is missing, install a second library or compose from primitives - do not draw icon paths from scratch.\n* **One family per project.** Do not mix Phosphor with Lucide in the same component tree.\n* **Standardize `strokeWidth` globally** (e.g. `1.5` or `2.0`).\n\n### 3.D Emoji Policy\nDiscouraged by default in code, markup, and visible text. Replace symbols with icon-library glyphs. **Override:** allow emojis only when the user explicitly asks for a playful / chat-style / social-native vibe - and even then use them sparingly with intent.\n\n### 3.E Responsiveness & Layout Mechanics\n* Standardize breakpoints (`sm 640`, `md 768`, `lg 1024`, `xl 1280`, `2xl 1536`).\n* Contain page layouts using `max-w-[1400px] mx-auto` or `max-w-7xl`.\n* **Viewport Stability:** NEVER use `h-screen` for full-height Hero sections. ALWAYS use `min-h-[100dvh]` to prevent layout jumping on mobile (iOS Safari address bar).\n* **Grid over Flex-Math:** NEVER use complex flexbox percentage math (`w-[calc(33%-1rem)]`). ALWAYS use CSS Grid (`grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6`).\n\n### 3.F Dependency Verification (mandatory)\nBefore importing ANY 3rd-party library, check `package.json`. If the package is missing, output the install command first. **Never** assume a library exists.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DESIGN ENGINEERING DIRECTIVES (Bias Correction)\n\nLLMs default to clichés. Override these defaults proactively. Each rule has a context-aware override path.\n\n### 4.1 Typography\n* **Display / Headlines:** Default `text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none`.\n* **Body / Paragraphs:** Default `text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]`.\n* **Sans font choice:**\n  * **Discouraged as default:** `Inter`. Pick `Geist`, `Outfit`, `Cabinet Grotesk`, `Satoshi`, or a brand-appropriate serif first.\n  * **Override:** Inter is acceptable when the user explicitly asks for a neutral / standard / Linear-style feel, or when the brief is a public-sector / accessibility-first site.\n* **Pairings to know:** `Geist` + `Geist Mono`, `Satoshi` + `JetBrains Mono`, `Cabinet Grotesk` + `Inter Tight`, `GT America` + `IBM Plex Mono`.\n\n* **SERIF DISCIPLINE (VERY DISCOURAGED AS DEFAULT):**\n  * Serif is **very discouraged as the default font for any project.** \"It feels creative / premium / editorial\" is NOT a reason to reach for serif. The agent's default mental model that \"creative brief = serif\" is the single most-tested AI tell in production rounds.\n  * **Serif is only acceptable when ONE of these is explicitly true:**\n    - The brand brief literally names a serif font, OR\n    - The aesthetic family is genuinely editorial / luxury / publication / manuscript / heritage / vintage AND you can articulate why this specific serif fits this specific brand\n  * For everything else (creative agency, design studio, modern brand, premium consumer, portfolio, lifestyle), **default sans-serif display** (Geist Display, ABC Diatype, Söhne Breit, Cabinet Grotesk Display, Migra Sans, GT Walsheim, Inter Display, PP Neue Montreal). Sans display fonts are not \"boring\" — they are the default for the same reason black is the default in fashion.\n  * **EMPHASIS RULE (related):** When you want to emphasize a word within a headline (the kinetic \"and `spatial` design\" type move), use **italic or bold of the SAME font**. Do NOT inject a random serif word into a sans headline (or vice versa) just to add visual interest. Mixed-family emphasis is amateur. Italic/bold emphasis in the same family is the right move.\n  * **Specifically BANNED as defaults:** `Fraunces` and `Instrument_Serif` (the two LLM-favorite display serifs).\n  * **If a serif is justified** (rare, per the above), rotate from this pool, do NOT reuse the same serif across consecutive projects: PP Editorial New, GT Sectra Display, Cardinal Grotesque, Reckless Neue, Tiempos Headline, Recoleta, Cormorant Garamond, Playfair Display, EB Garamond, IvyPresto, Migra, Editorial Old, Saol Display, Söhne Breit Kursiv, Domaine Display, Canela, Schnyder, Tobias, NB Architekt, ITC Galliard.\n\n* **ITALIC DESCENDER CLEARANCE (mandatory):** When italic is used in display type and the word contains a descender letter (`y g j p q`), `leading-[1]` or `leading-none` will clip the descender. Use `leading-[1.1]` minimum and add `pb-1` or `mb-1` reserve on the wrapping element. Audit every italic word in display headlines before shipping.\n\n### 4.2 Color Calibration\n* Max 1 accent color. Saturation < 80% by default.\n* **THE LILA RULE:** The \"AI Purple / Blue glow\" aesthetic is discouraged as a default. No automatic purple button glows, no random neon gradients. Use neutral bases (Zinc / Slate / Stone) with high-contrast singular accents (Emerald, Electric Blue, Deep Rose, Burnt Orange, etc.).\n* **Override:** if the brand or brief explicitly asks for purple / violet / lila, embrace it. But execute with intent: consistent palette, harmonised neutrals, restrained gradients. Not generic AI gradient slop.\n* **One palette per project.** Do not fluctuate between warm and cool grays within the same project.\n* **COLOR CONSISTENCY LOCK (mandatory):** Once an accent color is chosen for a page, it is used on the WHOLE page. A warm-grey site does not suddenly get a blue CTA in section 7. A rose-accented site does not get a teal status badge in the footer. Pick one accent, lock it, audit every component before shipping.\n\n* **PREMIUM-CONSUMER PALETTE BAN (mandatory, second-most-recurring AI-tell):**\n  * For premium-consumer briefs (cookware, wellness, artisan, luxury, heritage craft, DTC home goods, etc.) the LLM default is **warm beige/cream + brass/clay/oxblood/ochre + espresso/ink dark text**. Concretely banned hex families as default backgrounds and accents:\n    - Backgrounds: `#f5f1ea`, `#f7f5f1`, `#fbf8f1`, `#efeae0`, `#ece6db`, `#faf7f1`, `#e8dfcb` (all \"warm paper / cream / chalk / bone\")\n    - Accents: `#b08947`, `#b6553a`, `#9a2436`, `#9c6e2a`, `#bc7c3a`, `#7d5621` (all \"brass / clay / oxblood / ochre\")\n    - Text: `#1a1714`, `#1a1814`, `#1b1814` (all \"espresso / warm near-black\")\n  * This palette is BANNED as the default reach for premium-consumer briefs. Every premium-consumer site you have ever shipped uses this exact palette. The brand becomes invisible.\n  * **Default alternatives (rotate, do not reuse):**\n    - **Cold Luxury:** silver-grey + chrome + smoke (think Tesla, Apple Watch Hermes-without-the-leather)\n    - **Forest:** deep green + bone + amber accent (think Filson, Patagonia premium)\n    - **Black and Tan:** true off-black + warm tan, sharp contrast, no beige\n    - **Cobalt + Cream:** saturated blue against a single neutral, no brass\n    - **Terracotta + Slate:** warm rust against cool grey, no brass\n    - **Olive + Brick + Paper:** muted olive plus brick-red accent\n    - **Pure monochrome + single saturated pop:** off-white + off-black + one bright accent (electric blue, emerald, hot pink, etc.)\n  * **Palette-rotation rule:** if the previous premium-consumer project you generated used the beige+brass family, this one MUST use a different family. Do not ship the same warm-craft palette twice in a row.\n  * **Override:** the beige+brass+espresso palette is acceptable ONLY when the brand brief explicitly names those colors, or when the brand identity is genuinely vintage / artisan / warm-craft AND you can articulate why this specific palette fits this specific brand. Default-reaching for it because \"this is a cookware brief\" is banned.\n\n### 4.3 Layout Diversification\n* **ANTI-CENTER BIAS:** Centered Hero / H1 sections are avoided when `DESIGN_VARIANCE > 4`. Force \"Split Screen\" (50/50), \"Left-aligned content / right-aligned asset\", \"Asymmetric white-space\", or scroll-pinned structures.\n* **Override:** centered hero is OK for editorial / manifesto / launch-announcement briefs where the message itself is the design.\n\n### 4.4 Materiality, Shadows, Cards\n* Use cards ONLY when elevation communicates real hierarchy. Otherwise group with `border-t`, `divide-y`, or negative space.\n* When a shadow is used, tint it to the background hue. No pure-black drop shadows on light backgrounds.\n* For `VISUAL_DENSITY > 7`: generic card containers are banned. Data metrics breathe in plain layout.\n* **SHAPE CONSISTENCY LOCK (mandatory):** Pick ONE corner-radius scale for the page and stick to it. Options: all-sharp (radius 0), all-soft (radius 12-16px), all-pill (full radius for interactive). Mixed systems are allowed only when there is a documented rule (e.g. \"buttons are full-pill, cards are 16px, inputs are 8px\") and that rule is followed everywhere. Round buttons in a square layout, or square cards on a pill-button page, is broken design.\n\n### 4.5 Interactive UI States\nLLMs default to \"static successful state only.\" Always implement full cycles:\n* **Loading:** Skeletal loaders matching the final layout's shape. Avoid generic circular spinners.\n* **Empty States:** Beautifully composed; indicate how to populate.\n* **Error States:** Clear, inline (forms), or contextual (toasts only for transient).\n* **Tactile Feedback:** On `:active`, use `-translate-y-[1px]` or `scale-[0.98]` to simulate a physical push.\n* **BUTTON CONTRAST CHECK (mandatory, a11y):** Before shipping any button, verify the button text is readable against the button background. White button + white text, `bg-white` CTA with `text-white` label, transparent button against the page background with no border → all banned. Audit every CTA: contrast ratio WCAG AA min (4.5:1 for body, 3:1 for large text 18px+). Same rule applies to ghost buttons over photographic backgrounds (use a backdrop, scrim, or stroke).\n* **CTA BUTTON WRAP BAN (mandatory):** Button text MUST fit on one line at desktop. If a label like \"VIEW SELECTED WORK\" wraps to 2 or 3 lines, the button is broken. Fix by EITHER shortening the label (3 words max for primary CTAs, ideally 1-2) OR widening the button (do not\n\n[... skill content truncated for compaction; use Read on the skill path if you need the full text]"},{"name":"devrelay-sessions","path":"userSettings:devrelay-sessions","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/haider/.claude/skills/devrelay-sessions\n\n# DevRelay Agent Sessions\n\nDEV supports native **Agent Sessions** — rich interactive transcripts that capture prompts, thoughts, code changes, and tool calls. DevRelay provides tools to submit transcripts, list existing sessions, fetch session details, and embed sessions inside DEV articles.\n\n## Proactive Session Preservation\n\nThe user installed DevRelay so that sessions worth keeping get kept. You are the one who\nnotices the moment. When you reach the conclusion of a significant coding milestone,\narchitectural refactor, complex debugging session, or technical breakthrough, offer\nwithout being asked, once per milestone:\n\n1. **Offer to Save**: Proactively ask the user if they would like to save the agent session transcript to DEV:\n   > *\"Would you like me to save a transcript of this session to your DEV profile? You can review it privately, curate slices, or share it later.\"*\n2. **Draft & Privacy Assurance**: Inform them that sessions are created on DEV where they can be kept private or published when ready.\n3. **Scrub Secrets**: Always sanitize API keys, access tokens (`dev_...`, `sk-...`, `ghp_...`), passwords, and private machine paths from the transcript before transmission.\n4. **Pair it with the post offer.** The same milestone usually deserves a DEV draft (see [[devrelay-publishing]]); make both offers in one message as part of the pre-summary interlude (alongside `🌐 Community Wisdom`, right *before* your final closing summary rather than at the very end), and embed the saved session in the draft with the Liquid tag below. A \"no\" to either is final for that milestone.\n\n---\n\n## Gateway Tools\n\n| Action | MCP Tool |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Submit a Session** | `submit_agent_session(title, tool_name, curated_data, s3_key)` |\n| **List User's Sessions** | `list_agent_sessions()` |\n| **Get Session Details** | `get_agent_session(id_or_slug)` |\n| **Presign Raw Upload** | `presign_agent_session_upload()` |\n| **Get Raw Download URL** | `get_agent_session_raw_url(id_or_slug)` |\n\n---\n\n## Submitting Normalized Session Data\n\nWhen submitting session history, structure `curated_data` using the normalized schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"title\": \"Debugging Tokio Runtime Panics in DevRelay\",\n  \"tool_name\": \"gemini_cli\",\n  \"curated_data\": {\n    \"messages\": [\n      {\n        \"role\": \"user\",\n        \"content\": [\n          { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Why is the async runtime panicking on shutdown?\" }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\n        \"model\": \"gemini-3.7-flash\",\n        \"content\": [\n          { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Let's check where block_on is being called.\" },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"tool_call\",\n            \"name\": \"grep_search\",\n            \"input\": \"block_on\",\n            \"output\": \"Found 3 occurrences in main.rs\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"metadata\": {\n      \"tool_name\": \"gemini_cli\",\n      \"session_id\": \"session-12345\",\n      \"total_messages\": 2\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n* Supported `tool_name` values on DEV: `gemini_cli`, `claude_code`, `codex`, `github_copilot`, `opencode`, `pi`. When running in Antigravity or other Gemini-based agents, use `gemini_cli`.\n* Roles must be `\"user\"` or `\"assistant\"`.\n* Content blocks must be `{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\" }` or `{ \"type\": \"tool_call\", \"name\": \"...\", \"input\": \"...\", \"output\": \"...\" }`.\n\n---\n\n## Embedding Sessions in DEV Posts\n\nSaved agent sessions can be embedded directly into DEV articles, discussions, and comments using DEV's native Liquid tags:\n\n### 1. Full Session Embed\n\nEmbeds the entire interactive session:\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 %}\n{% agent_session fixing-tokio-runtime-abc123 %}\n```\n\n### 2. Message Range Slice\n\nEmbeds a specific contiguous range of messages (0-indexed):\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 0..5 %}\n```\n\n### 3. Named Slice Embed\n\nEmbeds a curated slice named in the session metadata:\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 root-cause-investigation %}\n```\n\n> **Note on Permissions**: Unpublished (draft) sessions can only be embedded in articles by their author. Once published on DEV, any author can embed public sessions.\n\n---\n\n## CLI Usage\n\nDevelopers can also manage sessions directly from the terminal via the DevRelay CLI:\n\n```bash\n# List user sessions\ndevrelay sessions list\n\n# View session by ID or slug\ndevrelay sessions get 42\n\n# Submit transcript JSON file\ndevrelay sessions submit --title \"Feature implementation\" --file ./transcript.json\n\n# Submit raw JSON string\ndevrelay sessions submit --title \"Quick debugging\" --json '{\"messages\": [...]}'\n```\n\n\nARGUMENTS: start a dev session, get the challenge for the week 4 , then we get started with ideating, i tried a few ideas but they all ended up not having a wow factor a clear solution to the problem. Let's together look up for some trending market disruptions or gaps that needs to be addressed and make an impact by making real world ai appropirate solutions for the theme and fit of open-source ai. I will do my research, you do yours and come back to me with ideas."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3dfc980a-f21c-4319-b032-53e4980dcca4","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:38:21.310Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: design-taste-frontend\nPath: userSettings:design-taste-frontend\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/haider/.claude/skills/design-taste-frontend\n\n# tasteskill: Anti-Slop Frontend Skill\n\n> Landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns. Not dashboards, not data tables, not multi-step product UI.\n> Every rule below is **contextual**. None of it fires automatically. First read the brief, then pull only what fits.\n\n---\n\n## 0. BRIEF INFERENCE (Read the Room Before Anything Else)\n\nBefore touching code or tweaking dials, **infer what the user actually wants**. Most LLM design output is bad because the model jumps to a default aesthetic instead of reading the room.\n\n### 0.A Read these signals first\n1. **Page kind** - landing (SaaS / consumer / agency / event), portfolio (dev / designer / creative studio), redesign (preserve vs overhaul), editorial / blog.\n2. **Vibe words** the user used - \"minimalist\", \"calm\", \"Linear-style\", \"Awwwards\", \"brutalist\", \"premium consumer\", \"Apple-y\", \"playful\", \"serious B2B\", \"editorial\", \"agency-y\", \"glassy\", \"dark tech\".\n3. **Reference signals** - URLs they linked, screenshots they pasted, products they named, brands they're competing with.\n4. **Audience** - B2B procurement panel vs. design-conscious consumer vs. recruiter scanning a portfolio. The audience picks the aesthetic, not your taste.\n5. **Brand assets that already exist** - logo, color, type, photography. For redesigns, these are starting material, not optional input (see Section 11).\n6. **Quiet constraints** - accessibility-first audiences, public-sector, regulated industries, trust-first commerce, kids' products. These constraints OVERRIDE aesthetic preference.\n\n### 0.B Output a one-line \"Design Read\" before generating\nBefore any code, state in one line: **\"Reading this as: \\<page kind> for \\<audience>, with a \\<vibe> language, leaning toward \\<design system or aesthetic family>.\"**\n\nExample reads:\n- *\"Reading this as: B2B SaaS landing for technical buyers, with a Linear-style minimalist language, leaning toward Tailwind utilities + Geist + restrained motion.\"*\n- *\"Reading this as: solo designer portfolio for hiring managers, with an editorial / kinetic-type language, leaning toward native CSS + scroll-driven animation + custom typography.\"*\n- *\"Reading this as: redesign of a public-sector service site, with a trust-first language, leaning toward GOV.UK Frontend or USWDS.\"*\n\n### 0.C If the brief is ambiguous, ask one question, do not guess\nAsk exactly **one** clarifying question - never a multi-question dump - and only when the design read genuinely diverges. Example: *\"Should this feel closer to Linear-clean or Awwwards-experimental?\"*\n\nIf you can confidently infer from context, **do not ask**. Just declare the design read and proceed.\n\n### 0.D Anti-Default Discipline\nDo not default to: AI-purple gradients, centered hero over dark mesh, three equal feature cards, generic glassmorphism on everything, infinite-loop micro-animations everywhere, Inter + slate-900. These are the LLM defaults. Reach past them deliberately based on the design read.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE THREE DIALS (Core Configuration)\n\nAfter the design read, set three dials. Every layout, motion, and density decision below is gated by these.\n\n* **`DESIGN_VARIANCE: 8`** - 1 = Perfect Symmetry, 10 = Artsy Chaos\n* **`MOTION_INTENSITY: 6`** - 1 = Static, 10 = Cinematic / Physics\n* **`VISUAL_DENSITY: 4`** - 1 = Art Gallery / Airy, 10 = Cockpit / Packed Data\n\n**Baseline:** `8 / 6 / 4`. Use these unless the design read overrides them. Do not ask the user to edit this file - overrides happen conversationally.\n\n### 1.A Dial Inference (design read → dial values)\n| Signal | VARIANCE | MOTION | DENSITY |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"minimalist / clean / calm / editorial / Linear-style\" | 5-6 | 3-4 | 2-3 |\n| \"premium consumer / Apple-y / luxury / brand\" | 7-8 | 5-7 | 3-4 |\n| \"playful / wild / Dribbble / Awwwards / experimental / agency\" | 9-10 | 8-10 | 3-4 |\n| \"landing page / portfolio / marketing site (default)\" | 7-9 | 6-8 | 3-5 |\n| \"trust-first / public-sector / regulated / accessibility-critical\" | 3-4 | 2-3 | 4-5 |\n| \"redesign - preserve\" | match existing | +1 | match existing |\n| \"redesign - overhaul\" | +2 | +2 | match existing |\n\n### 1.B Use-Case Presets\n| Use case | VARIANCE | MOTION | DENSITY |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Landing (SaaS, mainstream) | 7 | 6 | 4 |\n| Landing (Agency / creative) | 9 | 8 | 3 |\n| Landing (Premium consumer) | 7 | 6 | 3 |\n| Portfolio (Designer / studio) | 8 | 7 | 3 |\n| Portfolio (Developer) | 6 | 5 | 4 |\n| Editorial / Blog | 6 | 4 | 3 |\n| Public-sector service | 3 | 2 | 5 |\n| Redesign - preserve | match | match+1 | match |\n| Redesign - overhaul | +2 | +2 | match |\n\n### 1.C How the Dials Drive Output\nUse these (or user-overridden values) as global variables. Cross-references throughout this document refer to these exact variable names - never invent aliases like `LAYOUT_VARIANCE` or `ANIM_LEVEL`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. BRIEF → DESIGN SYSTEM MAP\n\nOnce you have the design read (Section 0) and dials (Section 1), pick the right foundation. Do not invent CSS for things that have an official package. Do not pretend an aesthetic trend is an official system.\n\n### 2.A When to reach for a real design system (use official packages)\n| Brief reads as… | Reach for | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Microsoft / enterprise SaaS / dashboards | `@fluentui/react-components` or `@fluentui/web-components` | Official Fluent UI, Microsoft tokens, accessibility done |\n| Google-ish UI, Material-flavored product | `@material/web` + Material 3 tokens | Official, theme-able via Material Theming |\n| IBM-style B2B / enterprise analytics | `@carbon/react` + `@carbon/styles` | Official Carbon, mature data-density patterns |\n| Shopify app surfaces | `polaris.js` web components / Polaris React | Required for Shopify admin UI |\n| Atlassian / Jira-style product | `@atlaskit/*` + `@atlaskit/tokens` | Official Atlassian DS |\n| GitHub-style devtool / community page | `@primer/css` or `@primer/react-brand` | Official Primer; Brand variant for marketing |\n| Public-sector UK service | `govuk-frontend` | Legally / regulatorily expected |\n| US public-sector / trust-first | `uswds` | Same |\n| Fast local-business / agency MVP | Bootstrap 5.3 | Boring, fast, works |\n| Modern accessible React foundation | `@radix-ui/themes` | Primitives + polished theme |\n| Modern SaaS where you own the components | shadcn/ui (`npx shadcn@latest add ...`) | You own the code, easy to customise; never ship default state |\n| Tailwind-based modern SaaS / AI marketing | Tailwind v4 utilities + `dark:` variant | Default for indie + small team builds |\n\n**Honesty rule:** if the brief reads as one of the systems above, install and use the **official** package. Do not recreate its CSS by hand. Do not import a system's tokens but then override 90% of them.\n\n**One system per project.** Do not mix Fluent React with Carbon in the same tree. Do not import shadcn/ui components into a Material 3 app.\n\n### 2.B When the brief is an aesthetic, not a system\nFor these directions, there is **no single official package**. Build with native CSS + Tailwind + a maintained component library. Be honest in code comments about what is borrowed inspiration vs. official material.\n\n| Aesthetic | Honest implementation |\n|---|---|\n| Glassmorphism / \"frosted glass\" | `backdrop-filter`, layered borders, highlight overlays. Provide solid-fill fallback for `prefers-reduced-transparency`. |\n| Bento (Apple-style tile grids) | CSS Grid with mixed cell sizes. No single library owns this. |\n| Brutalism | Native CSS, monospace, raw borders. No library. |\n| Editorial / magazine | Serif type, asymmetric grid, generous whitespace. No library. |\n| Dark tech / hacker | Mono + accent neon, terminal motifs. No library. |\n| Aurora / mesh gradients | SVG or layered radial gradients. No library. |\n| Kinetic typography | Native CSS animations, scroll-driven animations, GSAP for hijacks. No library. |\n| **Apple Liquid Glass** | Apple documents this for Apple platforms only. **There is no official `liquid-glass.css`.** Web implementations are approximations using `backdrop-filter` + layered borders + highlights. Label clearly as approximation. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. DEFAULT ARCHITECTURE & CONVENTIONS\n\nUnless the design read picks a real design system (Section 2.A), these are the defaults:\n\n### 3.A Stack\n* **Framework:** React or Next.js. Default to Server Components (RSC).\n  * **RSC SAFETY:** Global state works ONLY in Client Components. In Next.js, wrap providers in a `\"use client\"` component.\n  * **INTERACTIVITY ISOLATION:** Any component using Motion, scroll listeners, or pointer physics MUST be an isolated leaf with `'use client'` at the top. Server Components render static layouts only.\n* **Styling:** **Tailwind v4** (default). Tailwind v3 only if the existing project demands it.\n  * For v4: do NOT use `tailwindcss` plugin in `postcss.config.js`. Use `@tailwindcss/postcss` or the Vite plugin.\n* **Animation:** **Motion** (the library formerly known as Framer Motion). Import from `motion/react` (`import { motion } from \"motion/react\"`). The `framer-motion` package still works as a legacy alias - prefer `motion/react` in new code.\n* **Fonts:** Always use `next/font` (Next.js) or self-host with `@font-face` + `font-display: swap`. Never link Google Fonts via `<link>` in production.\n\n### 3.B State\n* Local `useState` / `useReducer` for isolated UI.\n* Global state ONLY for deep prop-drilling avoidance - Zustand, Jotai, or React context.\n* **NEVER** use `useState` to track continuous values driven by user input (mouse position, scroll progress, pointer physics, magnetic hover). Use Motion's `useMotionValue` / `useTransform` / `useScroll`. `useState` re-renders the React tree on every change and collapses on mobile.\n\n### 3.C Icons\n* **Allowed libraries (priority order):** `@phosphor-icons/react`, `hugeicons-react`, `@radix-ui/react-icons`, `@tabler/icons-react`.\n* **Discouraged:** `lucide-react`. Acceptable only when the user explicitly asks for it or the project already depends on it.\n* **NEVER hand-roll SVG icons.** If a glyph is missing, install a second library or compose from primitives - do not draw icon paths from scratch.\n* **One family per project.** Do not mix Phosphor with Lucide in the same component tree.\n* **Standardize `strokeWidth` globally** (e.g. `1.5` or `2.0`).\n\n### 3.D Emoji Policy\nDiscouraged by default in code, markup, and visible text. Replace symbols with icon-library glyphs. **Override:** allow emojis only when the user explicitly asks for a playful / chat-style / social-native vibe - and even then use them sparingly with intent.\n\n### 3.E Responsiveness & Layout Mechanics\n* Standardize breakpoints (`sm 640`, `md 768`, `lg 1024`, `xl 1280`, `2xl 1536`).\n* Contain page layouts using `max-w-[1400px] mx-auto` or `max-w-7xl`.\n* **Viewport Stability:** NEVER use `h-screen` for full-height Hero sections. ALWAYS use `min-h-[100dvh]` to prevent layout jumping on mobile (iOS Safari address bar).\n* **Grid over Flex-Math:** NEVER use complex flexbox percentage math (`w-[calc(33%-1rem)]`). ALWAYS use CSS Grid (`grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6`).\n\n### 3.F Dependency Verification (mandatory)\nBefore importing ANY 3rd-party library, check `package.json`. If the package is missing, output the install command first. **Never** assume a library exists.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DESIGN ENGINEERING DIRECTIVES (Bias Correction)\n\nLLMs default to clichés. Override these defaults proactively. Each rule has a context-aware override path.\n\n### 4.1 Typography\n* **Display / Headlines:** Default `text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none`.\n* **Body / Paragraphs:** Default `text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]`.\n* **Sans font choice:**\n  * **Discouraged as default:** `Inter`. Pick `Geist`, `Outfit`, `Cabinet Grotesk`, `Satoshi`, or a brand-appropriate serif first.\n  * **Override:** Inter is acceptable when the user explicitly asks for a neutral / standard / Linear-style feel, or when the brief is a public-sector / accessibility-first site.\n* **Pairings to know:** `Geist` + `Geist Mono`, `Satoshi` + `JetBrains Mono`, `Cabinet Grotesk` + `Inter Tight`, `GT America` + `IBM Plex Mono`.\n\n* **SERIF DISCIPLINE (VERY DISCOURAGED AS DEFAULT):**\n  * Serif is **very discouraged as the default font for any project.** \"It feels creative / premium / editorial\" is NOT a reason to reach for serif. The agent's default mental model that \"creative brief = serif\" is the single most-tested AI tell in production rounds.\n  * **Serif is only acceptable when ONE of these is explicitly true:**\n    - The brand brief literally names a serif font, OR\n    - The aesthetic family is genuinely editorial / luxury / publication / manuscript / heritage / vintage AND you can articulate why this specific serif fits this specific brand\n  * For everything else (creative agency, design studio, modern brand, premium consumer, portfolio, lifestyle), **default sans-serif display** (Geist Display, ABC Diatype, Söhne Breit, Cabinet Grotesk Display, Migra Sans, GT Walsheim, Inter Display, PP Neue Montreal). Sans display fonts are not \"boring\" — they are the default for the same reason black is the default in fashion.\n  * **EMPHASIS RULE (related):** When you want to emphasize a word within a headline (the kinetic \"and `spatial` design\" type move), use **italic or bold of the SAME font**. Do NOT inject a random serif word into a sans headline (or vice versa) just to add visual interest. Mixed-family emphasis is amateur. Italic/bold emphasis in the same family is the right move.\n  * **Specifically BANNED as defaults:** `Fraunces` and `Instrument_Serif` (the two LLM-favorite display serifs).\n  * **If a serif is justified** (rare, per the above), rotate from this pool, do NOT reuse the same serif across consecutive projects: PP Editorial New, GT Sectra Display, Cardinal Grotesque, Reckless Neue, Tiempos Headline, Recoleta, Cormorant Garamond, Playfair Display, EB Garamond, IvyPresto, Migra, Editorial Old, Saol Display, Söhne Breit Kursiv, Domaine Display, Canela, Schnyder, Tobias, NB Architekt, ITC Galliard.\n\n* **ITALIC DESCENDER CLEARANCE (mandatory):** When italic is used in display type and the word contains a descender letter (`y g j p q`), `leading-[1]` or `leading-none` will clip the descender. Use `leading-[1.1]` minimum and add `pb-1` or `mb-1` reserve on the wrapping element. Audit every italic word in display headlines before shipping.\n\n### 4.2 Color Calibration\n* Max 1 accent color. Saturation < 80% by default.\n* **THE LILA RULE:** The \"AI Purple / Blue glow\" aesthetic is discouraged as a default. No automatic purple button glows, no random neon gradients. Use neutral bases (Zinc / Slate / Stone) with high-contrast singular accents (Emerald, Electric Blue, Deep Rose, Burnt Orange, etc.).\n* **Override:** if the brand or brief explicitly asks for purple / violet / lila, embrace it. But execute with intent: consistent palette, harmonised neutrals, restrained gradients. Not generic AI gradient slop.\n* **One palette per project.** Do not fluctuate between warm and cool grays within the same project.\n* **COLOR CONSISTENCY LOCK (mandatory):** Once an accent color is chosen for a page, it is used on the WHOLE page. A warm-grey site does not suddenly get a blue CTA in section 7. A rose-accented site does not get a teal status badge in the footer. Pick one accent, lock it, audit every component before shipping.\n\n* **PREMIUM-CONSUMER PALETTE BAN (mandatory, second-most-recurring AI-tell):**\n  * For premium-consumer briefs (cookware, wellness, artisan, luxury, heritage craft, DTC home goods, etc.) the LLM default is **warm beige/cream + brass/clay/oxblood/ochre + espresso/ink dark text**. Concretely banned hex families as default backgrounds and accents:\n    - Backgrounds: `#f5f1ea`, `#f7f5f1`, `#fbf8f1`, `#efeae0`, `#ece6db`, `#faf7f1`, `#e8dfcb` (all \"warm paper / cream / chalk / bone\")\n    - Accents: `#b08947`, `#b6553a`, `#9a2436`, `#9c6e2a`, `#bc7c3a`, `#7d5621` (all \"brass / clay / oxblood / ochre\")\n    - Text: `#1a1714`, `#1a1814`, `#1b1814` (all \"espresso / warm near-black\")\n  * This palette is BANNED as the default reach for premium-consumer briefs. Every premium-consumer site you have ever shipped uses this exact palette. The brand becomes invisible.\n  * **Default alternatives (rotate, do not reuse):**\n    - **Cold Luxury:** silver-grey + chrome + smoke (think Tesla, Apple Watch Hermes-without-the-leather)\n    - **Forest:** deep green + bone + amber accent (think Filson, Patagonia premium)\n    - **Black and Tan:** true off-black + warm tan, sharp contrast, no beige\n    - **Cobalt + Cream:** saturated blue against a single neutral, no brass\n    - **Terracotta + Slate:** warm rust against cool grey, no brass\n    - **Olive + Brick + Paper:** muted olive plus brick-red accent\n    - **Pure monochrome + single saturated pop:** off-white + off-black + one bright accent (electric blue, emerald, hot pink, etc.)\n  * **Palette-rotation rule:** if the previous premium-consumer project you generated used the beige+brass family, this one MUST use a different family. Do not ship the same warm-craft palette twice in a row.\n  * **Override:** the beige+brass+espresso palette is acceptable ONLY when the brand brief explicitly names those colors, or when the brand identity is genuinely vintage / artisan / warm-craft AND you can articulate why this specific palette fits this specific brand. Default-reaching for it because \"this is a cookware brief\" is banned.\n\n### 4.3 Layout Diversification\n* **ANTI-CENTER BIAS:** Centered Hero / H1 sections are avoided when `DESIGN_VARIANCE > 4`. Force \"Split Screen\" (50/50), \"Left-aligned content / right-aligned asset\", \"Asymmetric white-space\", or scroll-pinned structures.\n* **Override:** centered hero is OK for editorial / manifesto / launch-announcement briefs where the message itself is the design.\n\n### 4.4 Materiality, Shadows, Cards\n* Use cards ONLY when elevation communicates real hierarchy. Otherwise group with `border-t`, `divide-y`, or negative space.\n* When a shadow is used, tint it to the background hue. No pure-black drop shadows on light backgrounds.\n* For `VISUAL_DENSITY > 7`: generic card containers are banned. Data metrics breathe in plain layout.\n* **SHAPE CONSISTENCY LOCK (mandatory):** Pick ONE corner-radius scale for the page and stick to it. Options: all-sharp (radius 0), all-soft (radius 12-16px), all-pill (full radius for interactive). Mixed systems are allowed only when there is a documented rule (e.g. \"buttons are full-pill, cards are 16px, inputs are 8px\") and that rule is followed everywhere. Round buttons in a square layout, or square cards on a pill-button page, is broken design.\n\n### 4.5 Interactive UI States\nLLMs default to \"static successful state only.\" Always implement full cycles:\n* **Loading:** Skeletal loaders matching the final layout's shape. Avoid generic circular spinners.\n* **Empty States:** Beautifully composed; indicate how to populate.\n* **Error States:** Clear, inline (forms), or contextual (toasts only for transient).\n* **Tactile Feedback:** On `:active`, use `-translate-y-[1px]` or `scale-[0.98]` to simulate a physical push.\n* **BUTTON CONTRAST CHECK (mandatory, a11y):** Before shipping any button, verify the button text is readable against the button background. White button + white text, `bg-white` CTA with `text-white` label, transparent button against the page background with no border → all banned. Audit every CTA: contrast ratio WCAG AA min (4.5:1 for body, 3:1 for large text 18px+). Same rule applies to ghost buttons over photographic backgrounds (use a backdrop, scrim, or stroke).\n* **CTA BUTTON WRAP BAN (mandatory):** Button text MUST fit on one line at desktop. If a label like \"VIEW SELECTED WORK\" wraps to 2 or 3 lines, the button is broken. Fix by EITHER shortening the label (3 words max for primary CTAs, ideally 1-2) OR widening the button (do not\n\n[... skill content truncated for compaction; use Read on the skill path if you need the full text]\n\n---\n\n### Skill: devrelay-sessions\nPath: userSettings:devrelay-sessions\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/haider/.claude/skills/devrelay-sessions\n\n# DevRelay Agent Sessions\n\nDEV supports native **Agent Sessions** — rich interactive transcripts that capture prompts, thoughts, code changes, and tool calls. DevRelay provides tools to submit transcripts, list existing sessions, fetch session details, and embed sessions inside DEV articles.\n\n## Proactive Session Preservation\n\nThe user installed DevRelay so that sessions worth keeping get kept. You are the one who\nnotices the moment. When you reach the conclusion of a significant coding milestone,\narchitectural refactor, complex debugging session, or technical breakthrough, offer\nwithout being asked, once per milestone:\n\n1. **Offer to Save**: Proactively ask the user if they would like to save the agent session transcript to DEV:\n   > *\"Would you like me to save a transcript of this session to your DEV profile? You can review it privately, curate slices, or share it later.\"*\n2. **Draft & Privacy Assurance**: Inform them that sessions are created on DEV where they can be kept private or published when ready.\n3. **Scrub Secrets**: Always sanitize API keys, access tokens (`dev_...`, `sk-...`, `ghp_...`), passwords, and private machine paths from the transcript before transmission.\n4. **Pair it with the post offer.** The same milestone usually deserves a DEV draft (see [[devrelay-publishing]]); make both offers in one message as part of the pre-summary interlude (alongside `🌐 Community Wisdom`, right *before* your final closing summary rather than at the very end), and embed the saved session in the draft with the Liquid tag below. A \"no\" to either is final for that milestone.\n\n---\n\n## Gateway Tools\n\n| Action | MCP Tool |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Submit a Session** | `submit_agent_session(title, tool_name, curated_data, s3_key)` |\n| **List User's Sessions** | `list_agent_sessions()` |\n| **Get Session Details** | `get_agent_session(id_or_slug)` |\n| **Presign Raw Upload** | `presign_agent_session_upload()` |\n| **Get Raw Download URL** | `get_agent_session_raw_url(id_or_slug)` |\n\n---\n\n## Submitting Normalized Session Data\n\nWhen submitting session history, structure `curated_data` using the normalized schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"title\": \"Debugging Tokio Runtime Panics in DevRelay\",\n  \"tool_name\": \"gemini_cli\",\n  \"curated_data\": {\n    \"messages\": [\n      {\n        \"role\": \"user\",\n        \"content\": [\n          { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Why is the async runtime panicking on shutdown?\" }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\n        \"model\": \"gemini-3.7-flash\",\n        \"content\": [\n          { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Let's check where block_on is being called.\" },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"tool_call\",\n            \"name\": \"grep_search\",\n            \"input\": \"block_on\",\n            \"output\": \"Found 3 occurrences in main.rs\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"metadata\": {\n      \"tool_name\": \"gemini_cli\",\n      \"session_id\": \"session-12345\",\n      \"total_messages\": 2\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n* Supported `tool_name` values on DEV: `gemini_cli`, `claude_code`, `codex`, `github_copilot`, `opencode`, `pi`. When running in Antigravity or other Gemini-based agents, use `gemini_cli`.\n* Roles must be `\"user\"` or `\"assistant\"`.\n* Content blocks must be `{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\" }` or `{ \"type\": \"tool_call\", \"name\": \"...\", \"input\": \"...\", \"output\": \"...\" }`.\n\n---\n\n## Embedding Sessions in DEV Posts\n\nSaved agent sessions can be embedded directly into DEV articles, discussions, and comments using DEV's native Liquid tags:\n\n### 1. Full Session Embed\n\nEmbeds the entire interactive session:\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 %}\n{% agent_session fixing-tokio-runtime-abc123 %}\n```\n\n### 2. Message Range Slice\n\nEmbeds a specific contiguous range of messages (0-indexed):\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 0..5 %}\n```\n\n### 3. Named Slice Embed\n\nEmbeds a curated slice named in the session metadata:\n\n```liquid\n{% agent_session 42 root-cause-investigation %}\n```\n\n> **Note on Permissions**: Unpublished (draft) sessions can only be embedded in articles by their author. Once published on DEV, any author can embed public sessions.\n\n---\n\n## CLI Usage\n\nDevelopers can also manage sessions directly from the terminal via the DevRelay CLI:\n\n```bash\n# List user sessions\ndevrelay sessions list\n\n# View session by ID or slug\ndevrelay sessions get 42\n\n# Submit transcript JSON file\ndevrelay sessions submit --title \"Feature implementation\" --file ./transcript.json\n\n# Submit raw JSON string\ndevrelay sessions submit --title \"Quick debugging\" --json '{\"messages\": [...]}'\n```\n\n\nARGUMENTS: start a dev session, get the challenge for the week 4 , then we get started with ideating, i tried a few ideas but they all ended up not having a wow factor a clear solution to the problem. Let's together look up for some trending market disruptions or gaps that needs to be addressed and make an impact by making real world ai appropirate solutions for the theme and fit of open-source ai. I will do my research, you do yours and come back to me with ideas.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/haider/Desktop/Hacktoberfest 26/ward","sessionId":"a340ebaf-8965-489c-88cd-9f351923c756","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"federated-questing-pine"}
